## Claim
Text: "COVID-19 pandemic pre-planned with the help from Bill Gates."
Claim author: Claire Edwards
Claim date: October 25, 2020
Claim origin: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xMLFiRmWSxk

## Initial Q&A
### Is there any credible, documented evidence that the COVID-19 pandemic was intentionally planned before SARS-CoV-2 emerged in late 2019?
Answer: No, the search result does not provide any credible evidence that the COVID-19 pandemic was pre-planned. Instead, it characterizes such claims as "unfounded" conspiracy theories, noting that while Bill Gates had previously warned about pandemic risks (such as in his 2015 TED Talk) and had personal preparedness plans, the CNBC article states: "some of whom have made unfounded claims that he's seeking to profit from the pandemic, or that he knew about coronavirus years before it appeared, or even that he wants to implant the global population with vaccine microchips." The article portrays Gates as someone who regrets not doing more to warn world leaders about pandemic dangers, not as someone who planned the pandemic.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/05/12/bill-gates-wishes-hed-done-more-to-warn-about-pandemic-danger.html

### Did Bill Gates or the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation play any role in creating, engineering, or releasing SARS-CoV-2?
Answer: According to the source, Bill Gates and the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation have been the subject of conspiracy theories that the article characterizes as "falsely linking him to the coronavirus' origins in some way or another." Gates himself called such theories "bizarre" and "so stupid or strange," and the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation's CEO Mark Suzman described the spread of such misinformation as "distressing." The source indicates these claims are baseless conspiracy theories, though it does not provide a specific direct statement addressing whether Gates was involved in creating, engineering, or releasing SARS-CoV-2 (it focuses more on the related microchip vaccine conspiracy theory).

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/06/05/bill-gates-responds-to-bizarre-covid-19-vaccine-conspiracy-theories.html

### What was the "Event 201" pandemic simulation held in October 2019, who organized it, and was it evidence of pre-planning for COVID-19 specifically?
Answer: "Event 201" was a pandemic simulation exercise held in October 2019, organized by the Johns Hopkins Center for Health Security in partnership with the World Economic Forum and the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation. It was not evidence of pre-planning for COVID-19; the organizers explicitly stated that it was "not a prediction," modeling a fictional coronavirus simply because "the world has seen a growing number of epidemic events" and "experts agree that it is only a matter of time before one of these epidemics becomes global." Previous similar exercises by the same organizations were not followed by outbreaks, and the choice of a coronavirus reflected scientific expertise given prior outbreaks like SARS and MERS, not foreknowledge of the specific virus that causes COVID-19.

Source URL: https://fullfact.org/health/event-201-coronavirus-pandemic/

### What were the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation's funding activities related to pandemic preparedness and coronavirus research prior to 2019?
Answer: Based on the search result, prior to 2019, the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation had engaged in several public health funding activities, including contributing "about $33 million" in 2009 to combat a tuberculosis outbreak in China and committing "as much as $10 billion to vaccine research over the next decade" in 2010. However, the search result does not provide specific details about the foundation's funding activities directly related to pandemic preparedness or coronavirus research prior to 2019, so a complete answer to that specific question cannot be determined from this source alone.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/02/06/business/bill-melinda-gates-foundation-novel-coronavirus/index.html

### What public statements did Bill Gates make about pandemic risks before the emergence of COVID-19?
Answer: Before COVID-19 emerged, Bill Gates made several high-profile public statements warning about pandemic risks, most notably at the 2017 Munich Security Conference. He stated that "a fast-moving airborne pathogen could kill more than 30 million people in less than a year" and that "there is a reasonable probability the world will experience such an outbreak in the next 10 to 15 years." He also referenced the 1918 flu pandemic, noting it "killed between 50 million and 100 million people," and warned that "the fact that a deadly global pandemic has not occurred in recent history shouldn't be mistaken for evidence that a deadly pandemic will not occur in the future." Additionally, Gates urged that "getting ready for a global pandemic is every bit as important as nuclear deterrence and avoiding a climate catastrophe."

Source URL: https://www.forbes.com/sites/brucelee/2017/02/19/bill-gates-warns-of-epidemic-that-will-kill-over-30-million-people/

### Have any governmental, intelligence, or independent scientific investigations concluded that COVID-19 was pre-planned?
Answer: No. The declassified U.S. Intelligence Community assessment on COVID-19 origins explicitly states the opposite — that the virus "was not developed as a biological weapon" and that "China's officials did not have foreknowledge of the virus before the initial outbreak." TheIC remains divided between a natural-exposure origin and a laboratory-associated incident (with no evidence of pre-planning in either scenario), and notes that "China's cooperation most likely would be needed to reach a conclusive assessment of the origins of COVID-19," indicating no investigation has reached a definitive conclusion of pre-planning.

Source URL: https://www.dni.gov/files/ODNI/documents/assessments/Unclassified-Summary-of-Assessment-on-COVID-19-Origins.pdf

### What is the current scientific consensus on the natural origin of SARS-CoV-2, and what evidence supports it?
Answer: The current scientific consensus is that SARS-CoV-2 has a natural zoonotic origin, with horseshoe bats of the genus *Rhinolophus* identified as the natural reservoir of sarbecoviruses. The source article states, "Present evidence indicates that *Rhinolophus* bats are the natural reservoir of all sarbecoviruses," and notes that a virus with 96% nucleotide identity to SARS-CoV-2 was discovered in *Rhinolophus affinis* collected in 2013 in Yunnan province, China. Supporting evidence also includes the detection of SARS-CoV-2-like viruses in Sunda pangolins (with 97.4% similarity in the receptor-binding domain of the spike protein, indicating they could infect human cells), the epidemiological link between early COVID-19 patients and the Huanan Seafood Wholesale Market in Wuhan, and the well-documented precedent of intermediate hosts in previous coronavirus outbreaks (masked palm civets for SARS-CoV 2002–2003, dromedary camels for MERS-CoV). The paper proposes that illegal wildlife trade likely facilitated viral transmission from bats to an intermediate host (possibly pangolins or small carnivores) and then to humans, rather than a laboratory origin.

Source URL: https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.1515/mammalia-2020-0044/html?lang=en

### When and where was the first known case of COVID-19 identified, and does the epidemiological timeline support a pre-planned scenario?
Answer: The first known cases of COVID-19 were reported to the WHO China Country Office on 31 December 2019, whencases of pneumonia of unknown etiology were detected in Wuhan City, Hubei Province of China, with 44 case-patients reported from 31 December 2019 through 3 January 2020. The epidemiological timeline does not support a pre-planned scenario, as it shows a reactive public health response: the causal agent was not identified during the initial reporting period, the novel coronavirus was only isolated on 7 January 2020, the genetic sequence was shared on 12 January 2020, and subsequent cases were traced to exposures in Wuhan—notably, the Huanan Seafood Wholesale Market was only closed on 1 January 2020 "for environmental sanitation and disinfection" after cases were already detected, demonstrating a discovery-driven rather than pre-orchestrated timeline.

Source URL: https://www.who.int/docs/default-source/coronaviruse/situation-reports/20200121-sitrep-1-2019-ncov.pdf

### Did Bill Gates have any documented foreknowledge of the specific nature, timing, or scale of the COVID-19 pandemic before it began?
Answer: No. According to the search result, there is no evidence that Bill Gates had documented foreknowledge of the specific nature, timing, or scale of the COVID-19 pandemic. While the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation co-sponsored "Event 201" in October 2019—a pandemic preparedness exercise—the fictional virus in that simulation was "modeled largely on SARS" and differed significantly from COVID-19 in its origins and severity. The Johns Hopkins Center for Health Security explicitly stated, "the Center for Health Security and partners did not make a prediction during our tabletop exercise," and that "the inputs we used for modeling the potential impact of that fictional virus are not similar to nCoV-2019." USA TODAY rated the claim that Event 201 predicted COVID-19 as FALSE. Bill Gates' prior warnings about pandemic risks (such as his 2015 TED Talk) were general statements about the threat of epidemics in general, not specific predictions about COVID-19.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/03/26/fact-check-bill-gates-backed-pandemic-exercise-didnt-predict-covid-19/5081854002/

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence gathered from multiple credible sources overwhelmingly refutes the claim that the COVID-19 pandemic was pre-planned with help from Bill Gates. Key findings include: (1) No credible evidence exists that the pandemic was pre-planned—multiple sources characterize such claims as "unfounded" conspiracy theories; (2) Bill Gates himself called such theories "bizarre" and "so stupid or strange"; (3) Event 201, co-sponsored by the Gates Foundation in October 2019, was explicitly stated by organizers to be "not a prediction," and USA TODAY rated the claim that it predicted COVID-19 as FALSE; (4) The U.S. Intelligence Community assessment explicitly states the virus "was not developed as a biological weapon" and that "China's officials did not have foreknowledge of the virus before the initial outbreak"; (5) The scientific consensus supports a natural zoonotic origin of SARS-CoV-2; and (6) The epidemiological timeline shows a reactive public health response beginning December 31, 2019, not a pre-orchestrated scenario.

## Conclusion

The evidence is comprehensive and unambiguous. Multiple independent sources—including U.S. intelligence assessments, scientific literature, fact-checking organizations, and statements from the organizers of Event 201 itself—explicitly and clearly refute the claim that the COVID-19 pandemic was pre-planned with help from Bill Gates. There is no supporting evidence for the claim, and substantial refuting evidence exists across all investigated angles.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
Multiple independent sources comprehensively refute the claim that the COVID-19 pandemic was pre-planned with help from Bill Gates. Such allegations are characterized as "unfounded" conspiracy theories, with Gates himself dismissing them as "bizarre" and "so stupid or strange" ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/06/05/bill-gates-responds-to-bizarre-covid-19-vaccine-conspiracy-theories.html)). The Gates Foundation–co-sponsored "Event 201" pandemic simulation (October 2019) was explicitly stated by organizers to be "not a prediction" modeling a fictional coronavirus, and USA TODAY rated the claim that it predicted COVID-19 as FALSE ([USA TODAY](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/03/26/fact-check-bill-gates-backed-pandemic-exercise-didnt-predict-covid-19/5081854002/); [Full Fact](https://fullfact.org/health/event-201-coronavirus-pandemic/)). The declassified U.S. Intelligence Community assessment concluded the virus "was not developed as a biological weapon" and that "China's officials did not have foreknowledge of the virus before the initial outbreak" ([ODNI](https://www.dni.gov/files/ODNI/documents/assessments/Unclassified-Summary-of-Assessment-on-COVID-19-Origins.pdf)). Scientific consensus supports a natural zoonotic origin of SARS-CoV-2, with bats identified as the natural reservoir and intermediate hosts (e.g., pangolins) likely facilitating transmission ([De Gruyter](https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.1515/mammalia-2020-0044/html?lang=en)). Finally, the epidemiological timeline—beginning with cases reported to WHO on December 31, 2019, isolation of the novel coronavirus on January 7, 2020, and genetic sequencing shared on January 12, 2020—reflects a reactive public health response, not a pre-orchestrated scenario ([WHO Situation Report](https://www.who.int/docs/default-source/coronaviruse/situation-reports/20200121-sitrep-1-2019-ncov.pdf)). Gates' prior warnings about pandemic risk (e.g., his [2015 TED Talk](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/05/12/bill-gates-wishes-hed-done-more-to-warn-about-pandemic-danger.html) and [2017 Munich Security Conference remarks](https://www.forbes.com/sites/brucelee/2017/02/19/bill-gates-warns-of-epidemic-that-will-kill-over-30-million-people/)) were general epidemic preparedness concerns, not specific foreknowledge of COVID-19.